#import "utils.typ": *

Information diffusion in social networks is an extremely challenging problem, in three very well defined aspects. First, the modelling and replication of human behaviour requires a high amount of quality and very specific data as well as sophisticated approaches in the reproducibility: the task is to describe and replicate a social behaviour, which is not easy. Secondly, the description is not of _one_ individual, but of a high quantity of them to generate the dynamics linking the collective and the individuals. Lastly, a great quantity of interactions and phenomena happen on microblogging social networks: user-to-user interaction, user-to-post interactions, bios, different feeds, replies and quotes, notifications... the system to describe is very extensive if the intended purpose is to do a one-to-one reproducibility. Let's address how this work has managed these three main roadblocks.

Regarding data acquisition, this project is only possible thanks to the ATProto implementation and the existence of Bluesky, as it has made available exact data of events that allow to measure certain parts of human behaviour on the social network ---such as which content is reposted (see @sec-data-firehose), obtaining of the network topology (see @sec-data-topology), and reconstruction of the structural virality with the `via` field (see @anx-data-via) --- and allowed to use methods to obtain non collected or not publicly available information ---such as sessions definitions (see @sec-method-session) and its appendix (see @apx-sessions) or posts per sessions (see @sec-cal-interaction). Despite Bluesky openness, this does not make the current available data optimal, in fact it is quite far away from it. If real session data from Bluesky and other networks were to be obtained, far more sophisticated analyses ---with probably more accurate results--- could be performed (see @sec-future).

Regarding the scalability, strong efforts to make the simulation performant have been made in this work: from the programming language election to the implementation itself (see @apx-impl) and simulation paradigm (Discrete-Event Simulation, see @sec-method-des) together with its design (see @sec-design). The simulation is capable of running a 12 hours 1 million users run in 18 real life minutes (see @sec-res-scalability) in a not dedicated server environment (see @apx-hardware) which is more than enough to be representative of the scalability, as well as allowing pretty fast iteration on the simulation itself and obtaining results.

Regarding system intrinsic complexity, some scope adjustments were needed to fit inside the scope of a master thesis work and managing all the complexity. First, a subset of features from Bluesky was taken into account to be modeled as the minimum skeleton of what a microblogging social network is (see @sec-sota-description and @sec-model), with three big feature omissions such as reply, quoting and notifications (see @apx-mechanics) in order to fit the scope. The subset of features can be described at high level as three possible user-post actions (repost, like or ignore) with a reverse-chonological timeline, excluding recomendation algorithms and impossibility of users interacting between each other; together with a Continuous-Time Independent Cascade with Activity driven nodes, this is the exact model presented and studied in this project.

Once the object to model was closed, some more simplifications were needed to not drown into the calibration complexity and implementation of the model: post homogeneity (treating posts as content-agnostic pods that propagate through the network) which ultimately implies user policy homogeneity over defined actions and a static network topology (no new users would join the simulation while it's active).

This work does not hide that, under all these simplifying assumptions, the implemented simulation can't produce the numbers of a fully-featured microblogging social network such as Bluesky, where the empirical data has been obtained. The results confirm this behaviour: all quantities are subpart with the emprical data; structual virality, the interested charechteristic magniture, is alsonot achived over the virality threshold, undrer representing also real data. In a nutshell, the system fails to reproduce the extreme behaviour of the characteristic long tail of social networks (see @sec-finding-missing-tail), but that does not discard the work of this project, but rathifies it as the shapes of the densities and behaviours are indeed similar, although far less extreme, making the heavy-tail charateristic of social networks not being reproduced.

In conclusion, this work set out to test whether a set of minimal mechanics of a microblogging platform are sufficient to reproduce the underlying mechanics of information diffusion and to generate similar cascade sizes, and of course this hypothesis does not hold: the missing features, deliberate trade-off for either scope or missing data steered this into replicating the global complex system of a microblogging social platform, although a negative hypothesis does not mean a failure, on the contrary: the construction succeeded end to end. *On measurability*, the user dynamics and a representative follower topology were recovered from openly available ATProto data at per-user resolution, as well as Structual Virality (RQ1). *On executability*, the discrete-event engine runs the model at the scale of the real network --- up to one million users --- in feasible time and memory while reaching a stationary regime (RQ2). *On core validity*, the calibrated core reproduces the shape class of the empirical cascades --- the same tiny, shallow, broadcast-dominated trees, with the lognormal repost distribution preferred in both --- but not their bulk: every size and width magnitude and the mean depth sit below the data, the non-trivial-cascade rate is roughly half the empirical one, and Structural Virality remains below the broadcast floor (RQ3). The simulated mechanisms therefore set the regime but not the scale. *On the boundary*, the departures from reality can be reasoned with modelling choices and with data and calibration artefacts, proving the limitations as a certain baseline: (RQ4) reasons are identified rather than residual, and collected in one place in @sec-future-boundary. Cascade depth is bounded by the subcritical reproduction number that content-agnostic behaviour imposes on every cascade (@sec-finding-missing-tail), and cascade width by a first hop capped jointly by an over-active followee population, a uniform conversion rate and in-network-only impressions (@sec-width-cause). The two caps are separated by mechanism but are not fully independent: the arrival rate also depresses the reproduction number, so correcting the inactive majority lifts the depth cap along with the width. The queue is tested and rejected as a cap (@sec-queue-attention), and the arrival-rate cap is an artefact of the calibrated population rather than of the model, so it is recoverable without a new mechanism. *On the specification of the missing mechanism*, together these findings translate into what the model lacks: (RQ5) post-level heterogeneity and impressions beyond the follower graph, independent of how they are implemented. That is what makes this a verified baseline rather than a finished model: the parts it does not explain are named, and named in the place where the next mechanism must enter. As such, it is an extremly solid first step into an accurate synthetic cascade simulator, to be extended with content-aware posts, some more features and a deeper analysis of the topology and user sampling with it. Achieving it would enable the study of hotter topics such as echo chambers, misinformation and democratic welfare in online platforms in a fully analyzable and potentially interventional manner.

